---
import ImageConverter from '../components/converter/ImageConverter';
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { absoluteUrl } from '../config/site';
import '../styles/image-workspace.css';

const title = 'PNG to STL Converter — Free Online Tool | IntoSTL';
const description = 'Convert PNG images into printable STL models in your browser. Transparent PNGs can be extruded, while opaque images can become reliefs.';
const questions = [
  { question: 'Does PNG transparency affect the STL?', answer: 'Yes. Meaningful transparency automatically selects Extrude, where the alpha channel defines the solid shape.' },
  { question: 'Should I use Relief or Extrude for a PNG?', answer: 'Use Relief when brightness should control height. Use Extrude when a clearly separated foreground should become a solid shape.' },
  { question: 'Are transparent pixels kept as empty space?', answer: 'In Extrude mode, transparent areas are excluded from the solid shape, so clear holes and gaps can remain open.' },
  { question: 'Is my PNG uploaded?', answer: 'No. IntoSTL reads and converts the PNG locally in your browser.' },
];
const structuredData = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: 'IntoSTL PNG to STL Converter', url: absoluteUrl('/png-to-stl/'), description, applicationCategory: 'UtilitiesApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any', isAccessibleForFree: true },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'BreadcrumbList', itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'IntoSTL', item: absoluteUrl('/') },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'PNG to STL', item: absoluteUrl('/png-to-stl/') },
  ] },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'FAQPage', mainEntity: questions.map(({ question, answer }) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: question, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: answer } })) },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/png-to-stl/" structuredData={structuredData}>
  <article class="tool-page">
    <header class="tool-intro shell-narrow">
      <h1>Convert PNG to STL</h1>
      <p>Turn a PNG into a printable relief or extruded model without uploading it.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="shell converter-wrap">
      <p class="privacy-note"><span aria-hidden="true">●</span> Private by design. Your PNG is processed locally in your browser.</p>
      <ImageConverter unifiedWorkspace client:load acceptedInput="png" uploadPrompt="Upload a PNG image" />
    </div>

    <nav class="on-this-page shell-narrow" aria-labelledby="png-page-nav">
      <p id="png-page-nav">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#png-how">How to convert PNG to STL</a></li>
        <li><a href="#png-transparency">Transparent PNGs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#png-style">Relief vs Extrude</a></li>
        <li><a href="#png-best">Best PNGs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#png-faq">FAQ</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="shell-narrow content-stack">
      <section aria-labelledby="png-how">
        <h2 id="png-how">How to convert PNG to STL</h2>
        <p class="section-intro">The PNG to STL converter reads the image locally and creates a printable relief or extrusion without uploading the file.</p>
        <ol class="steps">
          <li><span>1</span><div><strong>Choose a PNG</strong><p>Upload a PNG up to 20 MB and 4096 × 4096 pixels.</p></div></li>
          <li><span>2</span><div><strong>Preview the model</strong><p>Transparent artwork starts as Extrude; opaque artwork starts as Relief.</p></div></li>
          <li><span>3</span><div><strong>Download the STL</strong><p>Save the binary STL and check it in your slicer.</p></div></li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section class="two-column" aria-labelledby="png-transparency">
        <div><h2 id="png-transparency">How transparent PNGs become STL shapes</h2></div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>A transparent PNG contains an alpha channel that describes how visible each pixel is. When meaningful transparency is present, IntoSTL initially selects Extrude and uses that alpha-defined foreground to create a solid shape.</p>
          <p>Transparent areas are left outside the solid, so clear gaps and supported internal holes can remain open. This is direct alpha-channel handling, not background removal or vector tracing.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="two-column" aria-labelledby="png-style">
        <div><h2 id="png-style">Relief or Extrude for PNG?</h2></div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p><strong>Relief</strong> is the useful starting point for an opaque or photo-like PNG: pixel brightness becomes surface height. <strong>Extrude</strong> gives a clear foreground uniform depth.</p>
          <p>A high-contrast opaque image can also use Extrude, where a simple light-background threshold separates the foreground. You can change Style, Depth and Size before downloading.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="two-column" aria-labelledby="png-best">
        <div><h2 id="png-best">What PNG images work best?</h2></div>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Use a clean image with a clearly defined subject. Smooth, intentional transparency works better than faint edge noise, while strong tonal contrast helps an opaque PNG produce a readable relief.</p>
          <p>For artwork whose main purpose is an extruded mark, icon or brand shape, the <a href="/logo-to-stl/">logo to STL guide</a> has more focused preparation advice. For broader image choices, see <a href="/image-to-stl/">Image to STL</a>.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section aria-labelledby="png-faq">
        <h2 id="png-faq">PNG to STL FAQ</h2>
        <div class="faq-list">{questions.map(({ question, answer }) => <details><summary>{question}</summary><p>{answer}</p></details>)}</div>
      </section>
      <aside class="related-tool">
        <div><p class="related-heading">More image options</p></div>
        <div class="related-actions"><a class="secondary-button" href="/image-to-stl/">Image to STL</a><a class="secondary-button" href="/logo-to-stl/">Logo to STL</a></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
